# Quantum Lab — environment template
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# THIS FILE IS THE FULL LIST. It was rebuilt by grepping the source for
# `process.env.` (apps/web) and `os.environ` / `os.getenv` / pydantic Settings
# fields (services/*), then following each hit to the code that consumes it —
# not by copying a document. The same table lives in three places on purpose,
# and all three are derived from the same grep:
#
#   apps/web/lib/env.ts              the Next app's startup validator
#   services/api/app/core/env_check.py, services/agent/agent/env_check.py
#   docs/DEPLOYMENT.md § 2          the prose version, with where to get each
#
# If you add a new environment read anywhere, add it to this file and to the
# validator for that runtime. A variable the code reads and this file does not
# mention is the thing that makes a deployment mysterious.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EVERY VARIABLE BELOW IS OPTIONAL. There are no required ones.
#
# With an entirely empty environment the web app still runs in full: the lesson
# track, the circuit simulator, the eight visualizations, the challenges and the
# exams are self-contained TypeScript, and the tutor falls back to an offline
# answer set that says so. Each variable only unlocks something extra, and every
# consumer degrades rather than throws.
#
# What the startup validators DO fail on is a half-configuration — a combination
# that cannot work and that fails in silence. Those are called out below.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# BUILD TIME vs RUN TIME — the trap that costs the most hours.
#
# Every NEXT_PUBLIC_* name is inlined into the compiled output by the Next
# compiler, in server code as well as client code, and `next.config.mjs` does
# the same for NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL through its `env:` block. Their values are
# frozen at `next build`. Changing one in a hosting dashboard, with
# `docker run -e`, or in a compose `environment:` block does nothing at all:
# you must rebuild. docker-compose.yml passes them as build `args:` for exactly
# this reason. Every one is marked [BUILD] below.
#
# NEVER put a secret behind a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name. Those values are served to
# every visitor. The Supabase ANON key is fine — it is designed to be public and
# is protected by row-level security. The service-role key is not.


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Web app — the deployment's own identity
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [BUILD] Your real origin, e.g. https://quantumlab.example.
#
# Set this first, after your first deploy. Left unset it defaults to
# http://localhost:3000 and NOTHING BREAKS VISIBLY: canonical <link> tags, the
# sitemap and OG preview URLs all point at localhost, so search engines index
# localhost and social previews are blank — and Stripe checkout success/cancel
# URLs are built from the same value, so a customer who has just paid real money
# is redirected to a URL that is nothing on their machine.
NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=http://localhost:3000


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  AI tutor
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Unlocks live tutor answers in /tutor and in services/agent, and the two
# workbench tools that call a model (/workbench/generate and /workbench/evolve
# — the other eight need no key). Checked cheapest-first: Gemini's free tier,
# then Groq, then Anthropic, then Azure OpenAI, through the open-source
# LiteLLM / Vercel AI SDK gateways, so switching provider is one env var, not
# a code change. See docs/LLM-GATEWAY.md. lib/platform/llmConfig.ts holds the
# order; /admin/platform overlays all of it at runtime without a redeploy.
# Without any of the three the tutor answers from the built-in offline set and
# the status line in /tutor tells the learner which tier is actually live.

# Free tier — get one at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# Very cheap, very fast, open-weight models — get one at https://console.groq.com/keys
GROQ_API_KEY=

# Premium option — get one at https://console.anthropic.com → API Keys
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=

# Azure OpenAI — the fourth provider in the cheapest-first order, and the one
# this deployment shares with VegaDuta rather than paying for twice. Resource
# `vegaduta-ai-az-aoai` (eastus, S0), deployment `gpt-4-1-mini` (gpt-4.1-mini
# 2025-04-14, GlobalStandard pay-per-token). Key: Azure portal → that resource
# → Keys and Endpoint. Prefer pasting it at /admin/platform instead, where it
# is stored encrypted; this env layer is the fallback for a fresh deploy.
#
# AZURE_API_BASE is the resource's /openai root, NOT a full completions URL:
# app/api/agent/route.ts builds the client with `useDeploymentBasedUrls: true`,
# so the SDK appends /deployments/<deployment>/chat/completions itself.
# The deployment name goes in TUTOR_MODEL below — for Azure, the "model" IS
# the deployment name. All three of key, base and model must be set or
# providerReady() treats Azure as unconfigured and falls through. Caveat: in
# this env layer TUTOR_MODEL applies to EVERY provider, so once a second key
# is set, give that provider its own model at /admin/platform instead.
AZURE_API_KEY=
AZURE_API_BASE=https://vegaduta-ai-az-aoai.openai.azure.com/openai
# 2024-10-21 is what infra/azure/deploy.sh pins when it wires this resource,
# and what gpt-4.1-mini needs. Blank falls back to the AI SDK's own default.
AZURE_API_VERSION=2024-10-21

# ── On-device tutor (WebGPU) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to 1 to offer a tutor that runs entirely in the learner's browser: they
# download a small model once (376 MB / 879 MB / 2.2 GB, their choice), and
# after that every answer is free, has no message limit, and never leaves the
# device. Useful for three things this platform actually needs — an anonymous
# visitor's first question costs nothing, classrooms that may not send student
# text to a third-party API get a tutor at all, and it works with the network
# off, like the rest of the PWA.
#
# NEEDED AT BUILD TIME AND AT RUN TIME. At build, Next inlines it into the
# client bundle, which is what makes the panel render. At run, proxy.ts calls
# securityHeaders() per request, and that is what decides whether the CSP lets
# the browser reach huggingface.co for weights and allows wasm-unsafe-eval for
# the WebGPU compile. Set only one and it fails silently: build-only gives a
# button whose download CSP then blocks with no visible error. Off by default,
# and when off the CSP is byte-for-byte the closed policy it always was.
NEXT_PUBLIC_DEVICE_TUTOR=

# Pins one provider for everyone, ignoring the cheapest-first order. Set this
# to `azure` if you add a Gemini or Groq key later but still want Azure served.
FORCE_LLM_PROVIDER=

# LiteLLM/AI-SDK-style model id, e.g. gemini/gemini-2.5-flash,
# groq/llama-3.3-70b-versatile, anthropic/claude-sonnet-4-6 (services/agent),
# or gemini-2.5-flash / llama-3.3-70b-versatile / claude-sonnet-4-6 (web's
# direct-call tier, via TUTOR_MODEL below — its provider comes from which key
# above is set, not from a prefix). Leave unset for the cheap default.
MODEL=gemini/gemini-2.5-flash
TUTOR_MODEL=

# Where /api/agent finds services/agent — the tutor tier that can run circuits
# to check its own answers. Under docker compose this is http://agent:8001.
# Unset: the tutor drops to a direct model call, or to the offline set.
AGENT_SERVICE_URL=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Accounts and data (Supabase)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# HALF-CONFIGURATION IS AN ERROR: the app treats Supabase as configured only
# when BOTH the URL and the anon key are set. With one of the two, the app stays
# in local-only mode while you believe accounts are on. The Next validator
# refuses to start on that combination.
#
# Read supabase/README.md before running the schema files — and read
# docs/DEPLOYMENT.md § 6 first if you have ever sent an org invite, because
# re-running supabase/schema-orgs.sql DELETES every unclaimed invite.

# [BUILD] Supabase → Project Settings → API → Project URL
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=

# [BUILD] Same page → anon / public key. Safe in the browser; RLS protects it.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=

# Same page → service_role key. SERVER ONLY — it bypasses row-level security.
# Never rename this to NEXT_PUBLIC_*: that publishes it to every visitor, and
# the Next validator refuses to start if it finds such a name.
# Unlocks server-side writes: the Stripe webhook's subscription sync and org
# membership reads. Unset: entitlement lookups fall back to the free plan.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Health checks
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Gates the EXPENSIVE half of GET /api/health (`?deep=1`, which makes three
# real upstream calls) behind `x-health-token:` or `?token=`. Unset, deep
# checks stay open to anyone who can reach the route — bounded only by its
# rate limiter, not by a secret. A wrong or missing token never 401s; it just
# silently falls back to the cheap shallow report, so this can't be used to
# probe whether a token is configured. See docs/SECURITY.md, finding HEALTH-1.
HEALTH_DEEP_TOKEN=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Billing (Stripe)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Skip this whole section unless you are charging money. Without it every
# account is on the free plan and all free content is fully available.
#
# HALF-CONFIGURATION IS AN ERROR, and it is the most expensive one available
# here: plan resolution matches the price id on a Stripe subscription against
# the four STRIPE_PRICE_* names. An unset one matches nothing, so the webhook
# falls through to the free plan FOR SOMEONE WHO HAS JUST BEEN CHARGED, and
# existing subscribers are downgraded on their next renewal. Stripe reports
# success and so do your logs. Both the Next validator and
# scripts/preflight.mjs treat a secret key without all four price ids as a hard
# failure. Test mode and live mode have entirely separate keys and price ids.

# Stripe → Developers → API keys (sk_live_… / sk_test_…)
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# NOT on the API keys page. This whsec_… value is created together with the
# webhook endpoint — see docs/DEPLOYMENT.md § 5. Paste an sk_… here and every
# webhook fails signature verification while nothing looks like an outage.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# Stripe → Product catalogue → your product → Pricing (price_…)
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_PRO_YEARLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_TEAM_MONTHLY=
STRIPE_PRICE_TEAM_YEARLY=

# Bearer secret for POST /api/billing/reconcile, presented as
# `x-reconcile-secret`, letting an operator or a cron job re-pull a Stripe
# subscription's truth for ANY account (a signed-in user can already reconcile
# their own). Unset, that header is ignored outright — an empty secret must
# never authorise anything — so only the self-service path works, and the
# nightly sweep in docs/BILLING.md §8 has nothing to authenticate with.
BILLING_RECONCILE_SECRET=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Email (Resend)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Real outbound email: org invites, certificates. Unset, sendEmail returns
# { sent: false, reason } and prints the whole message — including a clickable
# accept URL — to the server console in development, so the invite flow is
# testable end to end with no provider account.
# https://resend.com → API Keys
RESEND_API_KEY=

# From-address on a domain you have verified in Resend, e.g.
# "Quantum Lab <hello@yourdomain.com>". Unset falls back to Resend's sandbox
# sender, which only delivers to your own address.
EMAIL_FROM=

# Bearer secret for POST /api/email/dispatch — one bounded pass of the 7-day
# course sender, meant to be invoked by an operator's cron (hourly is plenty).
# Same rules as BILLING_RECONCILE_SECRET: unset, the endpoint refuses every
# caller — an empty secret must never authorise anything. Note that setting
# this is necessary but NOT sufficient: dispatch also hard-refuses when the
# subscription store is the in-memory fallback (it forgets opt-outs on
# restart — docs/ONBOARDING.md §"Email"), so sending additionally needs
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY and RESEND_API_KEY.
EMAIL_DISPATCH_SECRET=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Search engine verification
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [BUILD] Proves domain ownership to Google Search Console without a DNS or
# file-upload step. Get it from search.google.com/search-console -> Settings ->
# Ownership verification -> HTML tag -> copy only the content="..." value (not
# the whole <meta> tag). Required before Search Console will show coverage
# data or accept a manual "request indexing" for this domain.
GOOGLE_SITE_VERIFICATION=

# [BUILD] Same proof for Bing Webmaster Tools (also feeds Yahoo, and
# DuckDuckGo via IndexNow). Get it from bing.com/webmasters -> Settings ->
# Verify ownership -> meta tag option -> copy only the content="..." value.
BING_SITE_VERIFICATION=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Error tracking (Sentry)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [BUILD] The single switch. With no DSN no Sentry code is loaded or bundled at
# all and the build is faster; the client init, the server init, the
# instrumentation hook and the withSentryConfig wrapper all read this one name,
# so there is no half-on state.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# [BUILD] Source-map upload targets. Ignored unless a DSN is set.
SENTRY_ORG=
SENTRY_PROJECT=

# [BUILD] Without it no upload is attempted and production stack traces stay
# minified. Set it in CI, not in a .env on your laptop.
SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# [BUILD] Release id attached to every error report so a minified stack trace
# maps back to a source map (uploaded under the same release name). The only
# one of the three that reaches the BROWSER bundle — server code can also read
# VERCEL_GIT_COMMIT_SHA or GITHUB_SHA, but those are invisible client-side, so
# on Vercel an unset value here means server events get a commit sha and
# client events — the ones with minified frames — get 'unknown'.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_RELEASE=

# [BUILD] Environment Sentry groups and filters by. Falls back to VERCEL_ENV,
# then NODE_ENV, then 'development'. Worth setting explicitly because
# NODE_ENV alone can't tell a preview deploy from production — both report
# 'production' — and an alert firing on a preview deploy at 2am is how people
# learn to ignore alerts.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_ENVIRONMENT=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Analytics
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [BUILD] Ships the local-first analytics buffer to a collector, and is added to
# the CSP connect-src. Unset, analytics stay in the browser and are never
# transmitted — a defensible default, and the one /insights is built on.
NEXT_PUBLIC_ANALYTICS_ENDPOINT=

# Server-side ingestion into that Supabase table. Needs the Supabase variables
# too. Unset, /api/analytics accepts and validates batches, stores nothing, and
# says so in the response. Columns: name text, at timestamptz, props jsonb,
# session_id text.
ANALYTICS_SUPABASE_TABLE=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Rate limiting — shared store (apps/web/lib/rateLimit.ts)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Guards routes that spend money per accepted request: /api/agent, /api/email,
# the Stripe checkout and portal routes, and the deep half of /api/health.
# Either pair below makes the limiter COUNT IN A SHARED STORE instead of one
# Node process's memory. On a serverless platform that in-memory fallback is
# close to "no limit at all" under load: every cold start begins with an empty
# counter and concurrent invocations do not share one — see the file's header
# comment for the full argument. Set ONE pair; the first one found wins.

# Upstash → your database → REST API section. Checked first.
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_URL=
UPSTASH_REDIS_REST_TOKEN=

# Vercel KV → Storage tab → .env.local snippet. Same REST dialect as Upstash
# (Vercel KV is Upstash underneath), so this pair is a drop-in alternative —
# set it instead of, not in addition to, the Upstash pair above.
KV_REST_API_URL=
KV_REST_API_TOKEN=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  services/api — circuit execution
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# [BUILD] Where the Next server's hardware proxy sends circuit work. Under
# docker compose this is http://api:8000 and it is passed as a BUILD ARG, not an
# environment variable — see the note at the top of this file. The browser never
# calls it directly; it goes same-origin through the Next proxy.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Comma-separated CORS origins accepted by services/api AND services/agent —
# one variable, both services. Set it to your real app origin in any deployment.
#
# `*` IS REFUSED AND THE SERVICE WILL NOT START. Both services send CORS
# responses with allow_credentials=true, and a wildcard origin combined with
# credentials lets any page on the internet call them from a visitor's browser.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000

# Backend used when a run request names none. A value that is not in this list
# makes every such request fail while /api/health stays green, so the API's
# startup validator refuses to start on an unknown id.
# Valid: aer_simulator, aer_noisy, cirq_simulator, braket_local, ibm_quantum,
#        ibm_brisbane, ionq_aria, rigetti_ankaa, azure_quantinuum_h1,
#        azure_ionq_aria
DEFAULT_BACKEND=aer_simulator

# OPT-IN authentication for services/api, which is otherwise ENTIRELY
# UNAUTHENTICATED — it runs user-supplied circuits and can spend IBM/Braket
# credits with no credential. Leave unset for local development.
#
# Set it and every request must carry `X-Quantum-Lab-Secret: <this value>`,
# except /api/health, which is exempt so a liveness probe still works.
# docker-compose.yml hands the same value to the web service as
# QUANTUM_API_SECRET so the Next proxy presents it.
#
# READ docs/SECURITY.md § "Exposing services/api safely" first.
API_SHARED_SECRET=

# The name the Next app reads for the same secret; API_SHARED_SECRET is accepted
# as an alias. UNDER DOCKER COMPOSE, SET ONLY API_SHARED_SECRET ABOVE — compose
# hands that one value to both services, and two names you have to keep in sync
# by hand is a 401 waiting to happen. This entry is for a split deployment
# (Vercel + Railway), where the web side sets this and the API side sets the
# other. Never a NEXT_PUBLIC_ variant: a secret a browser holds is not a secret.
QUANTUM_API_SECRET=

# A shared job store for services/api. Unset, jobs live in a bounded in-process
# dict: lost on restart, invisible to other replicas, so with more than one
# replica a poll can hit an instance that has never heard of the job. Requires
# `pip install redis`; under compose, `docker compose --profile queue up` and
# REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379.
REDIS_URL=


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Quantum hardware vendors — all optional, all degrade to "unavailable"
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Unlocks the ibm_quantum backend. Also needs `pip install qiskit-ibm-runtime`.
# https://quantum.ibm.com/
IBM_QUANTUM_TOKEN=

# The IBM Cloud service instance to address, as a CRN:
#
#   crn:v1:bluemix:public:quantum-computing:us-east:a/<account>:<guid>::
#
# OPTIONAL, and safe to leave blank on an account with exactly one instance.
#
# SET IT IF YOUR ACCOUNT HAS MORE THAN ONE — which is the normal shape once you
# have both an Open plan and a paid one. Leaving it blank is not an error and
# that is the problem: the SDK enumerates every instance the token can see and
# picks one. A job meant for the paid instance can land on the Open queue with
# a different allowance and a different bill, and a poll issued against the
# other instance answers "job not found" for a job that plainly exists.
#
# Find it in the IBM Quantum Platform console under your instance's details.
IBM_QUANTUM_INSTANCE=

# IBM Cloud region, when it is not the SDK's default (e.g. eu-de). Blank is fine.
IBM_QUANTUM_REGION=

# Unlocks the braket_local backend. Also needs `pip install amazon-braket-sdk`.
# HALF-CONFIGURATION IS AN ERROR: Braket reports itself available on the key id
# alone and then fails on every submission, so the API's validator refuses to
# start with a key id and no secret.
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_DEFAULT_REGION=us-east-1

# Alternate ways to satisfy the Braket availability check besides a key pair —
# a named CLI profile or an assumed-role session, read straight from the
# environment (not through Settings). Set at most one; unset, credential
# resolution falls through to AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID above, then to "unavailable".
AWS_PROFILE=
AWS_ROLE_ARN=

# Result destination for non-local Braket submissions.
BRAKET_S3_BUCKET=
BRAKET_S3_PREFIX=quantum-lab

# Azure Quantum workspace targeting.
AZURE_QUANTUM_RESOURCE_ID=
AZURE_QUANTUM_LOCATION=eastus


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  services/agent — tutor agent
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Where the agent's run_quantum_circuit tool sends work. Under docker compose
# this becomes http://api:8000. When it is unreachable the tool falls back to a
# LOCAL Aer simulation inside the agent process: answers stay correct, the CPU
# cost just lands on the agent.
QUANTUM_API_URL=http://localhost:8000

# Overrides the path of the committed course TF-IDF index. Unset uses
# services/agent/data/course_index.json. Retrieval fails OPEN — with no index
# the tutor still answers, just without the course under it, and nothing in the
# response says so — which is why the agent warns about it at startup.
QUANTUM_LAB_RAG_INDEX=

# Prompt-injection clamps. Defaults shown; all must stay positive or the agent
# refuses to start. They bound what an instruction hidden in a lesson, a paper
# abstract or a learner's QASM can spend. Raising them is a cost decision —
# read the comments in services/agent/agent/config.py first.
# MAX_TOOL_ROUNDS=6
# MAX_AGENT_SECONDS=60
# MAX_HISTORY_TURNS=20
# MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS=8000
# MAX_SHOTS=20000


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Escape hatch
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Downgrades every startup-validation ERROR to a warning, in all three runtimes.
# One legitimate use: a production incident where the validator is the thing
# keeping you from booting. Use it, then fix the configuration. It is not a
# setting; leaving it on permanently means the next silent misconfiguration
# ships without a word.
# QUANTUM_LAB_SKIP_ENV_CHECK=1
